## How It Works

A hash map uses an array of buckets and a hash function to map keys to indices. Collisions occur when two distinct keys produce the same index. Two primary resolution strategies exist: separate chaining and open addressing.

**Separate Chaining**: Each bucket stores a reference to a linked list or balanced tree of entries. On collision, the new entry is appended to the structure at that bucket. Lookup traverses the list comparing keys by equality. Java's HashMap uses this approach and converts a list to a balanced tree once it exceeds 8 entries, guaranteeing O(log n) worst-case lookup instead of O(n).

**Open Addressing**: All entries reside directly in the array. On collision, a probe sequence searches for the next empty slot. Common probing strategies include linear probing (index + 1, +2, +3), quadratic probing (+1, +4, +9), and double hashing. Python's dict uses open addressing with pseudo-random probing. Deletion requires tombstone markers because clearing a slot would break the probe chain for subsequent lookups.

## Code Example

```java
// Minimal separate chaining hash map
class HashMap<K, V> {
    private static class Node<K, V> {
        K key;
        V value;
        Node<K, V> next;
        Node(K key, V value) {
            this.key = key;
            this.value = value;
        }
    }

    private Node<K, V>[] table;
    private int capacity;

    public HashMap(int capacity) {
        this.capacity = capacity;
        table = new Node[capacity];
    }

    private int index(K key) {
        return Math.abs(key.hashCode()) % capacity;
    }

    public void put(K key, V value) {
        int i = index(key);
        Node<K, V> current = table[i];
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.key.equals(key)) {
                current.value = value;
                return;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        Node<K, V> n = new Node<>(key, value);
        n.next = table[i];
        table[i] = n;
    }

    public V get(K key) {
        int i = index(key);
        Node<K, V> current = table[i];
        while (current != null) {
            if (current.key.equals(key)) {
                return current.value;
            }
            current = current.next;
        }
        return null;
    }
}
```

## Key Points

- **Hash quality determines collision frequency**: A poor hash function amplifies collisions regardless of strategy
- **Load factor is the critical threshold**: Separate chaining tolerates high load gracefully while open addressing requires aggressive resizing above 70%
- **Cache locality differs fundamentally**: Open addressing stores entries contiguously while chaining follows pointer chains across heap memory
- **Deletion complexity varies**: Chaining is simple list removal while open addressing requires tombstones to preserve probe sequences

## Common Mistake

Using a mutable object as a hash map key after insertion. When an object's fields change after it has been placed in the map, the hash code changes as well. The map still holds the entry at the original bucket index computed from the old hash code. Subsequent lookups compute a different index, making the entry unfindable and creating a permanent memory leak. Java's HashMap and HashSet documentation explicitly warns against this, yet it remains one of the most common production bugs. The fix is to use only immutable keys (String, Integer, record classes) or ensure the fields contributing to hashCode are never modified after insertion.

## Interview Tip

The interviewer is testing whether you understand the mechanism behind O(1) average-case lookups, not just terminology. A good answer names both strategies and states one trade-off. A great answer connects strategy choice to real-world implementations: why Java trees at 8 entries (defend against hash-collision DoS attacks), why Python prefers open addressing (GC pressure from millions of small Node objects with chaining), and why a bad hashCode silently destroys performance with no warning.
